# Vocabulary shared by every noun: the scalars, and the kind-agnostic projections
# (Condition, Event, Schedule, ChangeType) each noun's surface reuses. Nothing here
# declares a root field, and a type only one noun uses belongs in that noun's file
# however generic its name reads. The root operation types are assembled from the
# per-noun files, which all `extend` them.

"An ISO-8601 encoded UTC date string."
scalar Time

"""
The identity of a persisted object (a cluster, a cluster's cache, …), opaque to
clients (a decimal-string id on the wire). One scalar for every object kind so
the same id machinery is reused. Use values returned by the server verbatim; do
not construct or parse them.
"""
scalar ObjectID

"""
An arbitrary JSON value carried verbatim. Backs a cached Kubernetes object's full
native body: the sidecar forwards the stored, decompressed object JSON as-is (no
per-field GraphQL typing), and the frontend casts it to a typed Kubernetes object.
"""
scalar JSON

"A condition's three-valued verdict, Kubernetes-style."
enum ConditionStatus {
  "The condition holds."
  True
  "The condition does not hold."
  False
  "The condition cannot currently be assessed."
  Unknown
}

"""
One Kubernetes-style status condition: an independently-tracked aspect of the
record's observed state. Connection-controller conditions (on `Cluster.conditions`):
`Connected` (the last probe reached the API server; reasons Connecting/
Connected/ResolveFailed/ProbeFailed/Inactive) and `Healthy` (the server's own
readiness; reasons Ready/ReadyzFailed/Unreachable/NoConnection/Inactive).
Sync-controller conditions: `Synced`, reported at two levels. On
`ClusterCache.conditions` it is coarse (reasons Syncing/Paused) — the cache states
only whether it decided to sync. The verdict a UI wants is the one on the cache's
sync children — the per-kind `ClusterCacheGVRSync` records (reasons
Syncing/Watching/Paused/NoConnection/SyncFailed/Stale), or their whole-cache rollup
`clusterCacheSyncHealthWatch`, which speaks the same vocabulary.
`ClusterCacheGVRDiscovery` reports its own axis on its own type: `Discovered` (reasons
Discovered/DiscoveryPartial/DiscoveryFailed/Paused/NoConnection). New types/reasons may appear; consumers should
treat unknown spellings as a degraded/unknown display state.
"""
type Condition {
  "The aspect this condition tracks, e.g. \"Connected\"."
  type: String!
  status: ConditionStatus!
  "CamelCase machine-readable explanation of `status`."
  reason: String!
  "Human-readable detail; empty when there is nothing to explain."
  message: String!
  """
  Whether this condition reports process-scoped state (a live connection, a running
  sync worker) rather than durable truth. A liveness condition written by a previous
  sidecar process reads as `Unknown` until the controller re-confirms it, so the UI
  never shows a stale `True` after a restart. `reason` is preserved across that
  downgrade, so it still says what the last known state was.
  """
  liveness: Boolean!
  """
  Whether `status` is a downgrade the sidecar derived rather than one a controller
  wrote: a liveness condition left behind by an earlier process, read as `Unknown`
  until re-confirmed. When true, `reason`, `message` and the stamps below describe
  the **last known** status, not this `Unknown` — so a consumer must not present
  them as current (no "Error" off a pre-restart reason, no "up for 0m").
  """
  unconfirmed: Boolean!
  "When `status` last changed — not when the condition was last refreshed. A downgraded liveness condition keeps the stamp of the write that set it, which predates the current process."
  transitionedAt: Time!
  "When the condition was last written, whether or not `status` changed."
  updatedAt: Time!
}

"An event's severity, mirroring the control plane's event type: Normal (✓) or Warning (✗)."
enum EventType {
  Normal
  Warning
}

"""
One coalesced run from an object's control-plane event timeline: consecutive
occurrences sharing an outcome (category, type, reason) are aggregated, so
`count`/`firstAt`/`lastAt` describe the run. A generic projection served under
every kind's events surface — `category` names the timeline (e.g. `"connection"`),
`reason` is an open, category-specific machine code; consumers should treat
unknown spellings as a degraded/unknown state. The client upserts a live stream
by `id` (a re-delivered id is an updated run; a new id is a new run).
"""
type Event {
  "Stable run identity — the client's upsert key on a watch. An opaque int64 id, same wire form as ObjectID."
  id: ObjectID!
  "The timeline this run belongs to, e.g. `\"connection\"`."
  category: String!
  type: EventType!
  "CamelCase machine code, e.g. `Connected` / `ProbeFailed`. Open-ended per category."
  reason: String!
  "The latest occurrence's detail; empty when there is nothing to explain."
  message: String!
  "Occurrences coalesced into this run (>= 1)."
  count: Int!
  "When the run started (first occurrence)."
  firstAt: Time!
  "When the run last extended (latest occurrence)."
  lastAt: Time!
}

"""
An object's reconcile-schedule gauge: when the control plane has queued the
object's next reconcile (a cluster's next backoff retry, for a disconnected one),
which a UI counts down to. Generic like Event — the watch entrypoints are
kind-scoped (clusterScheduleWatch) but the value shape is not. A struct (not a
bare Time) so it can grow. Observed live via a schedule watch — a scheduling
change fires no object/list watch, so this is the only way to keep the countdown
moving for an otherwise-idle object.
"""
type Schedule {
  "The scheduled time of the next reconcile, or null when nothing is scheduled."
  nextRequeueAt: Time
  """
  Whether a connection probe is running right now. Asserted by the controller for
  the in-flight window (not inferred from a missing nextRequeueAt), so the UI can
  show a definite "checking now" while the probe's round-trips are in flight.
  """
  probing: Boolean!
}

"""
Classifies a delta-watch change, mirroring a Kubernetes watch event. On subscribe a
watch replays the current set as `Added` changes (the snapshot), closes it with one
`Bookmark`, then streams live changes. A `Deleted` change carries the object's
last-known state; the client keys on its `id`.
"""
enum ChangeType {
  Added
  Modified
  Deleted
  """
  Closes the on-subscribe snapshot: sent exactly once per stream, after the last
  snapshot object and before the first live change. It carries **no object** — the
  change's entity field is null, the only case in which it is — so skip it rather
  than key on it. It is also what makes the collection fully known: a client must
  not render an empty state before it arrives. Kubernetes' `initial-events-end`
  bookmark, in this protocol's vocabulary.
  """
  Bookmark
}
